\title{Coherent Coordinate-Deletion Limits and\\Three All-Index Algebraic Packages}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We construct and classify the inverse limit of finite Boolean coordinate spaces $X_n=\mathbb F_2^{n+1}$ under maps deleting the final coordinate. Assembly from prefixes and extraction of final coordinates define mutually inverse additive maps between the limit and $\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$. Every finite-stage projection is therefore surjective, and every coordinate of a coherent family is transported from a unique diagonal coordinate. We place this theorem beside two independent all-index packages: the Bernoulli identity $B(t)(e^t-1)=t$ over $\mathbb Q$ and the nonvanishing of $w^n$ for all $n\geq0$ in $\mathbb F_2[w]$. The synthesis is structural, not identificatory: the three mechanisms encode infinitely many stages, coefficients, or degrees in one object. We contrast coordinate deletion with collapsing scalar towers, provide algorithms and complexity bounds, and formulate extensions to arbitrary abelian groups and completed graded algebras.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A recurring ambition in mathematics is to replace an infinite list of related objects by one object that contains them coherently. Inverse limits do this when the list carries maps from later stages to earlier stages. Formal generating functions do something parallel for indexed numerical sequences, and graded rings organize classes in every degree. Each construction converts infinitely many local statements into one global algebraic package.

One motivating image is a single ``mega-sphere'' whose projections recover $S^0,S^1,S^2,\ldots$. Literally, this is underdetermined. An inverse limit requires specified bonding maps $S^{n+1}\to S^n$, and ordinary consecutive spheres possess no canonical family of such maps. Different choices can produce different systems and limits. We isolate instead the precise combinatorial content suggested by the image: a stage with $n+1$ binary coordinates and a canonical map forgetting the newest coordinate.

Let $\mathbb F_2$ be the two-element field. We study
\[
\mathbb F_2\xleftarrow{p_0}\mathbb F_2^2
\xleftarrow{p_1}\mathbb F_2^3\xleftarrow{p_2}\cdots,
\]
where $p_n$ deletes the final coordinate. The main theorem identifies its inverse limit additively with $\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$. A sequence is assembled into finite prefixes; a coherent family is decoded by reading the last coordinate at each stage. Coherence supplies the transport identity making this diagonal complete.

Two further results broaden the all-index viewpoint. Bernoulli numbers are encoded by $B(t)(e^t-1)=t$. Separately, the generator $w$ in $\mathbb F_2[w]$, interpreted as a universal degree-one Stiefel--Whitney class, has a nonzero power in every degree. These are not invariants of the Boolean limit. They are complementary examples of infinite packaging, and keeping them distinct prevents unsupported geometric identifications.

\section{The coordinate-deletion system}

\begin{definition}[Finite Boolean stages]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, let
\[
X_n=\mathbb F_2^{\{0,1,\ldots,n\}}\cong\mathbb F_2^{n+1}.
\]
Define the additive bonding map
\[
p_n:X_{n+1}\longrightarrow X_n,
\qquad p_n(x_0,\ldots,x_n,x_{n+1})=(x_0,\ldots,x_n).
\]
For $m>n$, write $p_{n,m}$ for the composite deletion map $X_m\to X_n$.
\end{definition}

The composites satisfy $p_{n,m}p_{m,k}=p_{n,k}$ whenever $n<m<k$.

\begin{definition}[Inverse limit]
The inverse limit is the additive subgroup
\[
L=\left\{(v^{(n)})_{n\geq0}\in\prod_{n\geq0}X_n:
 p_n(v^{(n+1)})=v^{(n)}\text{ for every }n\right\}.
\]
Its elements are called coherent families. The stage projection is
$\pi_n:L\to X_n$, $\pi_n(v)=v^{(n)}$.
\end{definition}

The equations imply $p_{n,m}\pi_m=\pi_n$ for every $m>n$.

\begin{definition}[Assembly and diagonal]
For $a=(a_k)_{k\geq0}\in\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$, define
\[
A(a)^{(n)}=(a_0,\ldots,a_n).
\]
For $v\in L$, define
\[
D(v)_n=v^{(n)}_n.
\]
Thus $A:\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}\to L$ assembles prefixes, while
$D:L\to\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$ extracts final coordinates.
\end{definition}

Assembly is coherent because deleting $a_{n+1}$ returns the preceding prefix. Both maps are additive under coordinatewise addition.

\section{Transport and reconstruction}

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate transport]
For $v\in L$, $n\geq0$, and $0\leq i\leq n$,
\[
v^{(n)}_i=v^{(i)}_i=D(v)_i.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Apply the composite deletion map $p_{i,n}$ to $v^{(n)}$. Coherence gives $p_{i,n}(v^{(n)})=v^{(i)}$. Deletion leaves the $i$th coordinate unchanged, proving the first equality; the second is the definition of $D$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inverse-limit reconstruction]
Assembly and diagonal extraction are mutually inverse additive homomorphisms:
\[
D\circ A=\operatorname{id}_{\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}},
\qquad A\circ D=\operatorname{id}_L.
\]
Consequently $L\cong\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$ as additive groups.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The last coordinate of the stage-$n$ prefix of $a$ is $a_n$, so
$D(A(a))_n=a_n$. Conversely, at stage $n$ and coordinate $i$, the family
$A(D(v))$ has entry $D(v)_i$, which equals $v^{(n)}_i$ by coordinate transport. Hence all stages and coordinates agree. Additivity is coordinatewise.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Diagonal completeness]
Two coherent families $u,v\in L$ are equal if and only if $D(u)=D(v)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
One direction is immediate. For the other, apply $A$ and use $A(D(u))=u$ and $A(D(v))=v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonemptiness and cardinality]
The limit is nonempty and has the cardinality of the power set of $\mathbb N$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The zero sequence assembles to a point. Boolean sequences are precisely indicator functions of subsets of $\mathbb N$, and reconstruction gives a bijection with $L$.
\end{proof}

Although each $X_n$ is finite and their union is countable, the limit is uncountable: it contains one independent Boolean choice at every natural-numbered coordinate.

\section{Finite-stage recovery}

\begin{theorem}[Surjectivity of stage projections]
For every $n\geq0$, the projection $\pi_n:L\to X_n$ is surjective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given $x=(x_0,\ldots,x_n)$, define $a_i=x_i$ for $i\leq n$ and $a_i=0$ for $i>n$. Then $\pi_n(A(a))=x$.
\end{proof}

The zero extension is only one lift. Every choice of the tail gives another.

\begin{corollary}[Fibers]
For $x\in X_n$,
\[
\pi_n^{-1}(x)\cong\mathbb F_2^{\{n+1,n+2,\ldots\}}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Under $L\cong\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$, the condition $\pi_n(v)=x$ fixes exactly coordinates $0$ through $n$ and leaves the tail arbitrary.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Stage zero]
Since $X_0=\mathbb F_2$, either Boolean coordinate occurs at stage zero. Infinitely many coherent families project to each value.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Sparse pulse]
Fix $m$ and let $e^{(m)}_k=1$ if $k=m$ and $0$ otherwise. Then
$D(A(e^{(m)}))=e^{(m)}$. Stages below $m$ are zero, and every stage at or above $m$ contains one $1$ in position $m$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

For a finite sequence $(a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1})$, explicit prefix assembly produces
\[
(a_0),\ (a_0,a_1),\ldots,\ (a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1}).
\]
The output contains $N(N+1)/2$ entries, so independent materialization takes
$\Theta(N^2)$ time and space. Persistent shared prefixes reduce additional storage to $\Theta(N)$.

Coherence of explicit vectors $v^{(0)},\ldots,v^{(N-1)}$ is tested by comparing $v^{(n+1)}_i$ with $v^{(n)}_i$ for all $0\leq i\leq n<N-1$. Direct testing takes $\Theta(N^2)$ time, matching the explicit input size, and may stop at the first mismatch. Once coherence is known, diagonal decoding reads
\[
(v^{(0)}_0,v^{(1)}_1,\ldots,v^{(N-1)}_{N-1})
\]
in $\Theta(N)$ time and space. Reassembly is a canonical consistency check: transport guarantees exact reproduction for coherent inputs.

\section{Why bonding maps matter}

Nontrivial stages do not guarantee a rich inverse limit. Consider
\[
\mathbb Z\xleftarrow{\times2}\mathbb Z
\xleftarrow{\times2}\mathbb Z\xleftarrow{\times2}\cdots.
\]
A coherent family $(z_n)$ satisfies $z_n=2z_{n+1}$ and hence
$z_n=2^r z_{n+r}$ for all $r$.

\begin{proposition}[Collapse of the doubling tower]
The inverse limit of the integer doubling tower is the zero group.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For every $r$, the integer $z_n$ is divisible by $2^r$. The only such integer is zero. This applies to every $n$.
\end{proof}

Coordinate deletion is surjective and permits arbitrary extension. Multiplication by $2$ is not surjective and imposes increasingly severe divisibility. Compatibility can preserve freedom or extinguish it; the bonding maps decide which occurs.

\section{The Bernoulli package}

Let $\mathbb Q[[t]]$ be the formal power-series ring, with
\[
e^t=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{t^n}{n!}.
\]
Write
\[
B(t)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}B_n\frac{t^n}{n!}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Bernoulli generating identity]
There is a unique series $B(t)\in\mathbb Q[[t]]$ such that
\[
B(t)(e^t-1)=t.
\]
Equivalently, $B(t)=t/(e^t-1)$; its coefficients $B_n$ are the Bernoulli numbers.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Factor $e^t-1=tU(t)$, where
\[
U(t)=1+\frac{t}{2!}+\frac{t^2}{3!}+\cdots
\]
has constant coefficient $1$. It therefore has a unique inverse in $\mathbb Q[[t]]$. Set $B(t)=U(t)^{-1}$. Then $B(t)(e^t-1)=t$. Uniqueness follows from uniqueness of the inverse of $U$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bernoulli recurrence]
One has $B_0=1$, and for $m\geq2$,
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\binom{m}{k}B_k=0.
\]
Equivalently, for $n\geq1$,
\[
B_n=-\frac1{n+1}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\binom{n+1}{k}B_k.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The coefficient of $t^m/m!$ in $B(t)(e^t-1)$ is
$\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\binom{m}{k}B_k$. Compare it with the corresponding coefficient of $t$.
\end{proof}

The first values are
\[
B_0=1,\quad B_1=-\frac12,\quad B_2=\frac16,\quad B_3=0,
\quad B_4=-\frac1{30},\quad B_5=0,\quad B_6=\frac1{42}.
\]
No analytic convergence is involved. One formal identity controls all coefficients. The displayed triangular recurrence computes $B_0,\ldots,B_N$ using $\Theta(N^2)$ rational arithmetic operations and $\Theta(N)$ stored coefficients.

\section{Nonvanishing in every degree}

Let $\mathbb F_2[w]$ be graded by placing $w$ in degree $1$. In the standard universal projective-space model, $w$ represents a universal first Stiefel--Whitney class.

\begin{theorem}[All-degrees nonvanishing]
For every $n\geq0$, $w^n\neq0$ in $\mathbb F_2[w]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The coefficient of the degree-$n$ monomial in $w^n$ is $1$, whereas every coefficient of the zero polynomial is $0$. Since $1\neq0$ in $\mathbb F_2$, the polynomials differ.
\end{proof}

The untruncated ring matters. In $\mathbb F_2[w]/(w^{N+1})$, powers above degree $N$ vanish. Completion changes a different property: $1+w$ is not invertible in the polynomial ring, but in $\mathbb F_2[[w]]$,
\[
(1+w)^{-1}=1+w+w^2+w^3+\cdots.
\]
The product is $1$ because all positive-degree coefficients cancel in characteristic two. This distinction motivates a general theory of invertible total classes in completed graded algebras.

\section{Synthesis and interpretation}

\begin{theorem}[Three all-index packages]
The following hold simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The inverse limit of $X_n=\mathbb F_2^{n+1}$ under final-coordinate deletion is nonempty and additively isomorphic to $\mathbb F_2^{\mathbb N}$.
\item Every projection from the limit to a finite stage $X_n$ is surjective.
\item The Bernoulli series satisfies $B(t)(e^t-1)=t$ in $\mathbb Q[[t]]$.
\item Every $w^n$ is nonzero in $\mathbb F_2[w]$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first two claims are reconstruction and finite-stage recovery. The third is the Bernoulli generating identity, and the fourth is all-degrees nonvanishing. No identification among their ambient structures is required.
\end{proof}

The qualification is essential. Bernoulli numbers are not asserted to be homology groups of $L$, and $\mathbb F_2[w]$ is not asserted to be the cohomology ring of the Boolean limit. Nor have canonical maps among ordinary consecutive spheres been constructed. The valid synthesis concerns three modes of compression: coherent-stage compression, coefficient compression, and graded compression.

The sphere metaphor expresses an aspiration---one object with views in every dimension---while the coordinate model supplies a precise theorem. A genuinely homological replacement would require canonical truncations of a single stable object, rather than arbitrary maps among unstable spheres.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Prefix-consistent data.}
When each database version appends coordinates and restriction deletes the new data, a global state is exactly an infinite stream, while diagonal extraction reads the increment introduced at each version.

\paragraph{Symbolic dynamics.}
Boolean sequences model symbolic trajectories, and their finite prefixes are finite-time observations. The limit formalizes the equivalence between one infinite trajectory and all mutually compatible finite observations.

\paragraph{Multiresolution systems.}
If refinement adds independent coordinates and coarsening deletes them, every increment survives. More aggressive maps may impose constraints or collapse the compatible global state, as the doubling tower shows.

\paragraph{Exact arithmetic.}
The Bernoulli recurrence produces exact rational values used in power sums and asymptotic expansions. The generating identity is both a conceptual compression and a practical algorithm.

\paragraph{Characteristic-class bookkeeping.}
Polynomial powers model classes in successive degrees, truncation models finite-dimensional restrictions, and completion permits inversion of total classes with constant term one. Distinguishing these ambient algebras prevents invalid infinite manipulations.

\section{Generalizations and future work}

The reconstruction proof uses no multiplicative feature of $\mathbb F_2$. For any abelian group $A$, setting $X_n(A)=A^{n+1}$ and deleting the final coordinate should give a natural additive isomorphism
\[
\varprojlim_n A^{n+1}\cong A^{\mathbb N}.
\]
Naturality asks that every homomorphism $A\to A'$ commute with assembly, diagonal extraction, and stage projection.

For a scalar tower with nonzero integer multipliers $(d_n)$, coherent coordinates are divisible by successive partial products. A sharp criterion should relate triviality of the limit to unbounded absolute values of these products; bounded products can persist only when all but finitely many multipliers are units.

In a connected graded algebra over $\mathbb F_2$, every total class with constant coefficient one should become invertible in the degree completion, with inverse coefficients satisfying a universal convolution recurrence. This abstracts the geometric-series inverse of $1+w$.

A genuine bridge between limits and generating functions would begin with an inverse system of coefficient rings carrying compatible series. Under completeness and continuity hypotheses, the stagewise series should assemble uniquely over the inverse-limit ring, and coefficient extraction should commute with projection.

Finally, a homological successor to the sphere metaphor may be sought among truncations of a single connective spectrum. Such a construction would possess canonical bonding maps and could record stable contributions associated with spheres. Identifying the appropriate tower and computing its homotopy limit remain open tasks.

\section{Conclusion}

The coordinate-deletion tower is a complete model of one object storing every finite stage. Its inverse limit is the countable Boolean product; every finite vector lifts; and coordinate transport makes the diagonal a complete invariant. The proof is constructive and yields efficient finite algorithms.

The Bernoulli identity and universal polynomial powers offer two independent counterparts: one equation controls infinitely many rational coefficients, while one generator yields a nonzero class in every degree. Their common principle is architectural rather than geometric. Infinite families become tractable when compatibility, grading, or coefficient extraction is built into one algebraic object. The examples also mark the boundaries: unsuitable bonding maps collapse limits, truncation kills high powers, and completion changes invertibility. The result is not a literal sphere of all dimensions, but a precise account of how all-stage information can survive, be reconstructed, and be computed.

